    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d716">

                <pb facs="0679" n="581" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg581"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Choate.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-01-26"><hi rendition="#italic">January 26,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 286.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I transmit herewith copy of a letter from
                    Mr. Andrew J. Toomey, president of the Pennsylvania Milling and Export Company,
                    in regard to the company’s flour seized by the British naval authorities.</p>
                <p>I also inclose two copies of each of the invoices and bills of lading<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d716-f1"/> referred to in Mr. Toomey’s letter, one set of
                    which is for your own use and the other for the use of the British Government,
                    if in the exercise of your discretion you choose to make use of it.</p>
                <p>This Government approves Mr. Toomey’s views and suggestions as being just and
                    reasonable and as offering a sensible and simple solution of the questions as
                    regards the Pennsylvania Milling Company.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>






